WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A single particle interaction recorded a mile underground has physicists rethinking how dark matter might finally reveal itself. This is fascinating Frontiers, episode 181. An experiment located nearly a mile below ground in South Dakota captured one interaction that deviates from expected background signals. The facility searches for dark matter particles that make up roughly 85% of the universe's mass. Researchers note the anomaly could represent a

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:48.500
dark matter candidate or an unknown background process. Analysis continues to determine its origin. Follow-up runs will seek additional events of the same type. While physicists wait for confirmation of that possible dark matter event, other teams are using the early universe itself as a laboratory.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:01:13.640
Extreme quasars observed near cosmic dawn provide new constraints on black hole assembly time scales. These objects shine brightly enough to be seen across vast cosmic distances. Their properties help constrain formation time scales and growth rates. Models of early galaxy evolution now incorporate these observations. Additional spectra from large telescopes will refine the picture.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:34.380
Understanding how those black holes grew also benefits from the largest cosmological simulations yet produced. The Astrid Run models structure formation from the early universe to Z equals zero with high particle counts. Its resolution allows tracking of black hole mergers that feature gravitational wave observatories may detect.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:44.220
The simulation volume sits between smaller high resolution runs and larger lower resolution ones. Output catalogs are now available for the community.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:58.660
Analysts will use the data to guide observing strategies. Rainfall deficits combined with high temperatures have produced unusually dry conditions across much of the archipelago. Water shortages have already appeared in affected areas.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:08.580
Satellite observations from NASA Earth-observing instruments document the expanding dry zones. The data help monitor vegetation stress and reservoir levels.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:18.960
Continued monitoring will track whether the pattern persists into the fall months. Texas A&amp;M has entered the competition to operate NASA's jet propulsion laboratory.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:33.260
The laboratory manages multiple planetary and astrophysics missions. The bid process evaluates technical capability and institutional support. NASA will review proposals before selecting the next managing organization.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:43.260
The outcome will shape laboratory operations for the coming decade. Increasing numbers of missions raise the probability of detecting microbial life beyond Earth.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:55.640
Current international frameworks address planetary protection but leave gaps for security and response planning. The study explores how governments and agencies might coordinate if such a discovery occurs.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:04.220
Authors note that science fiction scenarios highlight real preparedness shortfalls. Further policy work is expected in coming years.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.640
Simulations like Astrid also help interpret real objects whose orbits are being rearranged right now.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:28.400
Centaur 450P slash Loneos has begun releasing gas and dust after a gravitational encounter with Saturn. The object orbits between Jupiter and Neptune yet now shows comet-like behavior. The activation occurred more than 3 billion miles from Earth.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:35.840
Observations track the developing coma and tail. Continued monitoring will reveal how long the activity persists.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:48.140
The same gravitational dynamics that stirred that centaur also shape the moon we see today. Southwest Research Institute modeling incorporates material strength during the Earth Moon forming collision.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:04:01.640
The calculations indicate an intact lunar body could assemble within roughly five hours. Earlier models without strength effects produced different outcomes. The new results may help narrow the timing of the impact event.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:20.320
Follow-on studies will test the findings against lunar sample data. Researchers examine 75 observed falls to identify the sequence of physical changes. The stages span initial heating, fragmentation, and final deceleration. Each phase leaves distinct signatures in recovered fragments.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:34.500
The work improves models used to predict ground impact locations. Additional falls will test and refine the framework. While models refine our picture of the moon's birth, hardware for exploring its surface is moving forward.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:52.040
The Magpie Rover Project moved forward during a signing ceremony in Copenhagen. The European Space Agency and ISpace Europe marked the start of implementation for what will become Europe's first lunar rover. The mission targets the moon's surface with a European build vehicle.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:05:00.280
Development will now proceed under the new agreement. Observers will watch for hardware milestones and launch scheduling in the coming years.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:18.240
Frownhofer IOF delivered a silicon-based double slit assembly and two high precision mirrors for the flex spectrometer. The satellite is scheduled for launch on September 15, 2026. From orbit, the instrument will measure sunlight-excited fluorescence from vegetation.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:29.000
The data will indicate photosynthetic activity and plant stress levels. Calibration of the optics is now complete. Commercial launch capability is also expanding.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:41.000
As a new Chinese vehicle just completed its debut flight. The first test flight of galactic energies, Zhishan Xing one vehicle, lifted off at 0200 UTC on September 1.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:56.340
The launch reportedly carried a dummy payload that reached orbit successfully. Confirmation now awaits tracking data from US sensors. The vehicle is also known as Palace One and represents a new entrant in China's commercial launch sector.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:06.700
Next steps include verification of orbital parameters and any follow-on test flights. Another commercial player is preparing infrastructure farther out on the way to Mars.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:19.860
Blue Origin received a firm fixed price contract with a maximum value of approximately $700 million. The award covers development of a Mars telecommunications orbiter to support future missions.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:32.720
The spacecraft will provide high bandwidth communications and navigation services. Delivery is expected within the contract period. This selection adds a new commercial provider to Mars infrastructure planning.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:43.960
The spacecraft continues to return data after emerging from its latest hibernation period. Two mission extensions have allowed solar system science beyond the original Pluto encounter.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:55.380
Proposed NASA budget reductions could end the heliospheric component of the mission as early as October. The craft has already studied the Jupiter system Pluto and Arokath.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.440
Project teams are assessing which instruments might remain active.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:12.760
Toyohiro Akiyama flew aboard the MIR space station in 1990 as a reporter for Tokyo Broadcasting System. His mission marked the first time a Japanese citizen and a working journalist reached orbit.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:26.380
Akiyama documented life aboard the station during the flight. Colleagues remember his contributions to public understanding of space flight. His passing closes a notable chapter in early international space participation.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:39.760
Picture an object the size of a small mountain drifting in the cold between Jupiter and Neptune. If you could stretch a measuring tape from Earth to that body, the distance would equal roughly 4.8 billion kilometers.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:52.540
Or more than 30 times the span from here to the Sun. That vast separation keeps the surface frozen solid for billions of years until a rare close pass by Saturn rearranges its orbit and drops it inward.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:08:04.020
Once the distance to the Sun shrinks enough for sunlight to warm the ice's, trapped gases begin to sublimate, lifting dust and creating the hazy envelope we recognize as a comet's coma.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:14.640
The transition can unfold over months rather than years because the thermal pulse travels only meters into the porous surface before vapor pressure overcomes the weak gravity.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:22.920
Yet the precise trigger, whether it is the first warming cycle or cumulative effects from earlier distant encounters, remains unknown.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:28.920
Leaving observers to wonder why some send tours awaken while others stay inert at the same distance.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:39.460
Keep an eye on follow-up observations of 450 p-slash loneos as its activity continues. That covers today's space and science news.

